## One source, many outputs

Write once in plain text and choose the output in the front matter. Every format
shares the same block model, the offline math and highlighting, and the live edit
loop, so anything you learn for one carries to the rest.

::: {.feature-grid}
::: {.feature}
### Blog posts & papers
Prose, math, figures, citations, and cross-references, with executable Python/R
cells and live interactive graphics.

[See an example →](formats.tmd#blog-posts)
:::

::: {.feature}
### Multi-chapter books
A sidebar, numbered chapters, cross-chapter references, and full-text search. These
very docs are a Taliesin book.

[Read the docs book →](docs/guide/)
:::

::: {.feature}
### Multi-page websites
Navbar, footer, OpenGraph, Cmd-K search, and opt-in post listings with an Atom feed per
dated listing. This whole site is a Taliesin website, built by the same `build` you run.

[How this site is built →](formats.tmd#websites)
:::
:::

## Edit the code. The output re-runs in place.

A `{python}` cell runs against a warm kernel. Change a parameter and only
that cell (and the figure it produced) re-renders. The rest of the page never
moves, and the kernel never cold-starts.

<figure class="tali-figure">
<video src="assets/live-code-dark.mp4" controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
       aria-label="Screencast"></video>
<figcaption>One value changes in the source; the matplotlib figure updates in place, scroll preserved.</figcaption>
</figure>

## Built the way you would build yours

There is nothing here a screenshot could fake: **this landing page, the surface you
spun at the top of it, and the [documentation book](docs/guide/) it links to are all
built and served by Taliesin**, from plain `.tmd` files in one repository. If it reads
well here, the framework is doing its job.

::: {.hero}
## Write your next thing in plain text

Author in `.tmd`, preview it live, and ship self-contained HTML: a post, a paper, a
book, or a site.

